Z||000000||SLAMMED87GT||12-17-2001||02:58 PM||||How about the Ground Control coil-overs as far as ride quality and ease of adjustability? They can be adjusted with the wheels in place, no? <P>I know some of you have them. I am looking to do that on my '87 GT to replace the rear springs. <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Pfierosig2_1.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>3" Drop, 17" Primax 618s/Nitto450 205/40, 4" Monza exhaust, Mudstain decklid scoop, 7" wing stands, Clear front and rear lights, custom interior, all options...that's all hehe.||||reg||6
Z||000001||Slammed Fiero||12-17-2001||03:10 PM||||Ride Quality??? haha<P>Depends on what rate you go with , the stiffer the better for handling purposes. Yes they can be adjusted with the wheels on the car.<P>I run 325lbs rate in back , anyone who has ridden in will agree there is little or no ride quality. But I don't really car about the ride.<P>a 290lbs rear setup will be good. Keep in mind I have Koni's outback whaich makes the rear even stiffer.<P>All in all a good investment. I would change a few things on the gorund controls , but thats a whole other thread.<P>JM <P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, Big Bore TB, 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
